evidence.quote:
Henceforth, until the induction is closed, assume Theorem <a href="#thm:main" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:main">1</a> in dimensions below $n$, and fix $$\label{cone:eq-induction}
 n=N+1\ge5,\qquad
 d(x,X)\ge p_n,$$ with $x$ singular.

For the fixed cone and large-stabilizer construction, $$\begin{aligned}
 d(o,C)
 &\leq
 \frac{\bigl((N/n)\ell+A'/n\bigr)^n}
 {\bigl(\ell^{n/N}+(h/\sigma)^{1/N}\bigr)^N}
 \label{boot:optimized}\\
 &\leq (N/n)^n+(A'/n)^n\sigma/h
 \leq \frac{p_n}{2}+\frac{M_n}{2}.
 \label{boot:bootstrap-inequality}
\end{aligned}$$ Equality in the middle, Hölder, inequality holds precisely when $$\label{boot:holder-equality}
 (A')^N\ell\,\frac{\sigma}{h}=N^N.$$

outputs.exact_statement:
The following assertions hold.*

1.  *$\Gamma$ is an additive semigroup with one-dimensional leaves, and its generated group is exactly $\Lambda$. Every finite compatible Taylor jet is realized by sections of all sufficiently large degrees in each admissible progression.*



2.  *$K$ is a full-dimensional closed convex cone, contains the positive constant ray, projects onto the whole exponent orthant, and satisfies $K=\overline{\mathop{\mathrm{int}}K}$. Its bounded-degree sections are bounded. More precisely, uniformly in the chosen point and nearby grading, $$\label{jet:bezout-bound}
     |\gamma|:=\sum_i\gamma_i\le C_C\frac jr
                           \qquad ((j,\gamma)\in K).$$ Its scaled image has the form $$\label{jet:epigraph}
     \widetilde K=\{(D_0,x):x\in\mathbb R_{\ge0}^N,\ D_0\ge f(x)\},$$ where $f$ is finite, positively homogeneous, convex, and $$\label{jet:f-coercive}
     f(x)\ge c_C|x|\qquad(x\in\mathbb R_{\ge0}^N)$$ for a constant $c_C>0$ independent of the nearby grading and chosen point.*


3.  *In the scaled coordinates the lattice density is $s$. If $\ell$ is a linear functional strictly positive on $\widetilde K\setminus\{0\}$, then $$\label{jet:count}
     \begin{aligned}
     &\#\{\gamma'\in\widetilde\Gamma:\ell(\gamma')<q\}\\
     &\qquad=s\,\operatorname{Leb}\{(D_0,x)\in\widetilde K:\ell(D_0,x)<1\}\,q^n+o(q^n),
     \end{aligned}$$ with Lebesgue measure in the scaled coordinates. No saturation assumption on $\Gamma$ is required.*


4.  *The vector $$\label{jet:canonical-vector}
     c_0=(r,\mathbf1)\qquad\text{in the original }(j,\gamma)
     \text{ coordinates}$$ belongs to $\Lambda$. Its scaled coordinates are $(N,\mathbf1)$.

For $\delta\in\mathbb Q_{\ge0}$, the rule $$\label{jet:test-rule}
 v_\delta\Bigl(\sum_j s_j\Bigr)
   =\min_{s_j\ne0}\left\{\frac{Nj}{r}
                           +\delta\mathop{\mathrm{ord}}_z(s_j)\right\}$$ defines a valuation centered at the vertex. It is divisorial up to positive rescaling, and $$\begin{aligned}
 A_C(v_\delta)&=N+N\delta,\label{jet:test-discrepancy}\\
 A_C(v_\delta)^n\mathop{\mathrm{vol}}(v_\delta)
   &=N^n(1+\delta)^n s
        \int_{\mathbb R_{\ge0}^N}e^{-f(x)-\delta|x|}\,dx.
                                      \label{jet:test-volume}
\end{aligned}$$

For $u=4/3$ one has $$\label{prob:universal-bound}
 \sup_{N\ge1}\ \sup_{\substack{a\in\mathbb R^N\\\sum_i a_i=0}}
 \frac{e^u}{u}\mathbb E g(u+a\cdot X)
 =C_*:=\frac34e^{4/3}-\frac e3<2.$$ The supremum over dimensions is approached by the vectors $(2,-2/(N-1),\ldots,-2/(N-1))$ as $N\to\infty$.

Fix $u>0$ and $N\ge2$. Every nonzero finite local maximum of $$a\longmapsto\mathbb E g\left(u+\sum_{i=1}^N a_iX_i\right),
 \qquad \sum_i a_i=0,$$ has, after permutation, the form $$\label{prob:exceptional-vector}
 a=(t,-t/(N-1),\ldots,-t/(N-1))\qquad(t>0).$$

For every sufficiently large grading in the subsequence under consideration, $$\label{jet:interior-conclusion}
 c_0=(r,\mathbf1)\in\mathop{\mathrm{int}}K
                 \qquad\text{in the original degree coordinates}.$$ Equivalently, $f(\mathbf1)<N$. For an exact minimizing rational grading with $m\ge r/N$, the conclusion holds without passage to a limit.

The algebra $\overline S$ is finitely generated, normal, Cohen–Macaulay, and Gorenstein. Its canonical module has a homogeneous generator of bidegree $(r,N)$. There is a section $v\in S_m$ whose initial $\overline v$ is $\tau^m$. In particular $T(v)=0$, and $$\label{filt:laurent-product}
 \overline S[\overline v^{-1}]
 \simeq \mathbb C[\overline v,\overline v^{-1},u_1,\ldots,u_N],
 \qquad T(u_i)=1,\quad T(\overline v)=0.$$ The ring $D=\overline S/(\overline v)$ is an equidimensional Cohen–Macaulay Gorenstein ring of dimension $N$, with $$\label{filt:D-series}
 \mathop{\mathrm{Hilb}}_T D(z)=(1-z)^{-N},
 \qquad \deg\omega_D=(r-m,N).$$ Its $T$-degree-zero piece is $\mathbb C$. A bihomogeneous vector-space basis of $D$ can be lifted to sections $e_i\in S$ such that, on putting $T_i=T(e_i)$, $$\label{filt:S-basis}
 \{v^a e_i:a\in\mathbb N\}$$ is a vector-space basis of $S$, compatible with original degree and $T$-order. The unique vector of this basis with $a+T_i=0$ is $1$.

Suppose $F$ respects original degree, has finite length in each original homogeneous piece, and $\mathop{\mathrm{gr}}_F S$ is finitely generated. Suppose also that $\mathfrak X=\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}R_F$ is normal and Gorenstein, and that $\mathop{\mathrm{gr}}_F S$ is Cohen–Macaulay and Gorenstein with a canonical generator of bidegree $(r,Q)$. Then $R_F$ is finite type and its absolute canonical module has a homogeneous generator of bidegree $(r,Q-1)$, which on $\lambda\ne0$ is $$\label{filt:canonical-form}
 \Omega_{\mathfrak X}
   =c\lambda^{-Q}\Omega_C\wedge d\lambda,
 \qquad c\in\mathbb C^*.$$ For a quasi-monomial valuation $V$ centered on $\mathfrak X$ over $\lambda=0$, put $\ell=V(\lambda)>0$ and $w=V|_{\mathbb C(C)}$. If $w$ is centered at $o$, then $$\label{filt:discrepancy-inequality}
 A_C(w)\leq A_{\mathfrak X}(V)+(Q-1)\ell.$$ The same inequality holds whenever the discrepancies involved are finite and defined by valuation retractions.

The filtration $W$ is multiplicative, with $$\label{filt:second-graded}
 G=\mathop{\mathrm{gr}}_WS\simeq D[\mathtt v],\qquad
 \deg_W\mathtt v=1,\qquad \deg_W|_D=T.$$ Here $\mathtt v$ is a regular polynomial variable of original degree $m$. Moreover $$\label{filt:G-series}
 \mathop{\mathrm{Hilb}}_WG(z)=(1-z)^{-n},\qquad
 \deg\omega_G=(r,n).$$ The $W$-Rees space is a finite-type normal Gorenstein variety. Its canonical generator has bidegree $(r,n-1)$ and, on $\lambda\ne0$, is $c\lambda^{-n}\Omega_C\wedge d\lambda$.

For the minimizing slice valuation and its restriction, $$\label{boot:refined-volume}
 \mathop{\mathrm{vol}}(w)\leq
 \min_{0\leq x\leq1}
 \left\{\frac{x^n}{\ell^n}
                 +(1-x)^n\frac{\sigma}{h}\right\}.$$

For the fixed cone and large-stabilizer construction, $$\begin{aligned}
 d(o,C)
 &\leq
 \frac{\bigl((N/n)\ell+A'/n\bigr)^n}
 {\bigl(\ell^{n/N}+(h/\sigma)^{1/N}\bigr)^N}
 \label{boot:optimized}\\
 &\leq (N/n)^n+(A'/n)^n\sigma/h
 \leq \frac{p_n}{2}+\frac{M_n}{2}.
 \label{boot:bootstrap-inequality}
\end{aligned}$$ Equality in the middle, Hölder, inequality holds precisely when $$\label{boot:holder-equality}
 (A')^N\ell\,\frac{\sigma}{h}=N^N.$$

Let $x\in X$ be a klt hypersurface germ of dimension $n$, with equation $F$ in regular ambient parameters $z_0,\ldots,z_n$. For positive rational weights $w_0,\ldots,w_n$, write $d_F$ for the weighted order of $F$. Then $$\label{end:eq-hypersurface-test}
 \widehat{\operatorname{vol}}(x,X)\le
           \frac{d_F\bigl(\sum_{i=0}^n w_i-d_F\bigr)^n}
                {\prod_{i=0}^n w_i}.$$
